const info=[
{t:'Step 1: White Sunlight',x:'Sunlight looks white, but it\'s actually a mixture of all visible colors — every wavelength from violet to red. Each color has a different wavelength, which turns out to be the key to everything that follows.',f:'💡 Red light has the longest wavelength (~750 nm), while violet has the shortest (~380 nm). All these colors travel together through space, but water has a way of separating them.'},
{t:'Step 2: Refraction',x:'When sunlight enters a raindrop, it slows down because water is denser than air. This change in speed causes the light to bend — a process called refraction. The droplet acts like a tiny prism.',f:'🔬 The refractive index of water is about 1.33, meaning light travels roughly 33% slower in water than in a vacuum.'},
{t:'Step 3: Dispersion',x:'Water bends different colors by different amounts. Violet bends the most, red the least. This wavelength-dependent refraction — called dispersion — splits white light into a spectrum.',f:'🎨 This is also why you can use a glass prism to make a rainbow. The angle difference between violet and red is about 2° inside the water.'},
